        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Dosimetry measurements are necessary for quantifying the exposure and risk received by crewmembers due to space flight exposure to ionizing radiation. Measurements throughout the crew compartment provide the data necessary to estimate exposure in the event of loss or damage of crew dosimeters and to validate and/or improve models used to calculate crew exposures. The purpose of this DSO is to compare the space flight dosimeter calibration techniques of the NASA Space Radiation Analysis Group (SRAG) and Russian Institute for Biomedical Problems (IBMP) to identify systematic errors between the two; correlate the independent, simultaneous space radiation exposure measurements and calculations made by the two space programs; and assess the radiobiological effects of high-charge, high-energy particles on actively metabolizing biological samples. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Crewmembers wore two crew passive dosimeters (CPDs), one U.S. and one Russian, for the duration of the flight. The 12 passive radiation dosimeters (PRDs) were affixed to the crew compartment at the six predefined locations preflight. The area passive dosimeters (APDs) remained in the locker for the duration of the flight. The Tissue Equivalent Proportional Counter (TEPC) was affixed to the port side of the middeck and powered on. The seed containers were affixed to the detector portion of the TEPC. The Active Autonomous Dosimeter (AAD) was affixed to the spectrometer portion of the TEPC. On the last flight day (FD), the TEPC was powered down and the locker stowed. The seed containers and the AAD were returned to the locker.
<p>
All hardware was supplied by SRAG and the IBMP. The U.S. operational passive dosimeters are routinely affixed in the orbiter preflight using a velcro map. The Russian PRDs were affixed next to the U.S. passive dosimeters using the same velcro map. 
<p>
The operational control dosimeters were shipped to the Kennedy Space Center (KSC) with the flight hardware, but were not placed in the orbiter. The control dosimeters were returned to the Johnson Space Center (JSC) after the landing with the flight dosimeters.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results for this experiment are currently unavailable.
        </div>

